# Example code for demonstrating Bates numbering using the Apryse SDK
The code should be read in conjunction with the blog

You will need a license key. You can get a trial license key from https://docs.apryse.com/core/guides/get-started/trial-key

Place the key into an Environment Variable called APRYSE_SDK_KEY

## Apply-bates-stamp.py
Creates a Bates number as a stamp. Currently, it will be red (just to make it visible). You can also adjust the rotation.

## Apply-bates-stamp-as-annot.py
Uses stamper to create an annotation.
Currently it sets the Author to "Bates" and displays the ID of the annotation.

## Apply-bates-freetext-annotation.py
Creates a Freetext annotation with the Author as "Bates".

## Apply-bates-freetext-annotation.py
Shrinks, and centers, the existing page content so that there is a margin into which the Bates number can be placed.

## Apply-bates-freetext-annotation.py
Creates Bates numbers as annotations then flattens all annotations in the document.

## Flatten-just-bates-annotations.py
Takes an existing PDF that contains Bates numbering and other annotations, flattens the Bates numbers and reapplies the other annotations.

## Apply-bates-stamp-and-certify.py
Adds a Bates number then adds a certifying signature to allow tamper detection.
Verfies that the generated file has not been tampered with, and that a manually edited file reveals that is has been tampered with.

## Sample files
- 042338.pdf taken from the GovDocs1 collection. https://digitalcorpora.org/corpora/file-corpora/files/
- NoMargins.pdf. Taken from Apryse Samples Testfiles, then modified to have no margins.
- output_freetext-added-annots.pdf. Generated by Apply-bates-freetext-annotation.py, then manually edited to have extra annotations.
- 042338_bates_certified-hidden-widget-edited.py. Generated by Apply-bates-stamp-and-certify.py, then edited using Firefox to remove a page.

- apryse.cer and apryse.pfx are taken from the Apryse Samples Testfiles

